WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:12.200
Picture a shop's program that sends the hour's refunds all at once: 40 requests in one second, at the end of every hour, and nothing in between.

2
00:00:12.367 --> 00:00:13.531
Is that an attack?

3
00:00:13.531 --> 00:00:15.471
No, just a program in a hurry.

4
00:00:15.471 --> 00:00:20.064
But a broken program that sends 40 every second, forever, is a problem.

5
00:00:20.064 --> 00:00:24.980
How do you write one limit that lets the first through and stops the second?

6
00:00:25.133 --> 00:00:28.127
The limits so far count requests per window.

7
00:00:28.127 --> 00:00:31.393
A limit of 5 a minute refuses most of the burst.

8
00:00:31.393 --> 00:00:35.884
A limit of 40 a second lets the broken program flood you, forever.

9
00:00:36.033 --> 00:00:40.257
One number per window can't say: bursts are fine, floods are not.

10
00:00:40.257 --> 00:00:45.391
What we want sounds like this: a burst of up to 10, then 2 a second on average.

11
00:00:45.567 --> 00:00:50.149
Picture a jar of tokens on a counter, like tokens for an arcade.

12
00:00:50.149 --> 00:00:54.732
It holds at most 10, and gains one token every 500 milliseconds.

13
00:00:54.900 --> 00:00:56.860
Each request takes a token.

14
00:00:56.860 --> 00:01:00.055
If the jar is empty, the request is refused.

15
00:01:00.267 --> 00:01:03.687
Map it: a token is permission for one request.

16
00:01:03.687 --> 00:01:06.141
The jar's size, 10, is the burst.

17
00:01:06.141 --> 00:01:10.676
One new token every 500 milliseconds is the rate: 2 a second.

18
00:01:10.867 --> 00:01:14.027
The program was quiet, so its jar is full.

19
00:01:14.027 --> 00:01:16.208
It sends 15 requests at once.

20
00:01:16.208 --> 00:01:17.412
How many get in?

21
00:01:17.412 --> 00:01:18.616
Pause and guess.

22
00:01:21.867 --> 00:01:22.177
Ten.

23
00:01:22.177 --> 00:01:26.360
It spends the whole jar, and the last 5 find it empty.

24
00:01:26.567 --> 00:01:31.887
Now it sends one request every 250 milliseconds, for 5 seconds.

25
00:01:31.887 --> 00:01:33.491
That's 20 requests.

26
00:01:33.491 --> 00:01:34.843
How many get in?

27
00:01:35.400 --> 00:01:35.715
Ten.

28
00:01:35.715 --> 00:01:41.866
A new token arrives every 500 milliseconds, so every second request finds one.

29
00:01:41.866 --> 00:01:46.833
Refused, let in, refused, let in: 2 a second, exactly the rate.

30
00:01:47.867 --> 00:01:51.813
So who drops a token into the jar every 500 milliseconds?

31
00:01:51.813 --> 00:01:53.543
A timer for every client?

32
00:01:53.543 --> 00:01:59.151
With a million clients, that's a million timers ticking for jars nobody is using.

33
00:01:59.367 --> 00:02:01.381
The trick is a lazy shopkeeper.

34
00:02:01.381 --> 00:02:05.865
He tops up the jar only when someone walks in, by checking the clock.

35
00:02:06.067 --> 00:02:18.233
Store two numbers per client in a Redis hash, a small table of named fields under one key: tokens, what was left after the last request, and ts, when the jar was last topped up.

36
00:02:18.367 --> 00:02:26.976
On each request, work out the tokens earned since ts: the time gone by, divided by 500 milliseconds, rounded down.

37
00:02:26.976 --> 00:02:29.921
Add them, never above 10, and take one.

38
00:02:30.100 --> 00:02:32.216
Watch it after 5 quiet seconds.

39
00:02:32.216 --> 00:02:35.084
Nothing moved while the program was quiet.

40
00:02:35.267 --> 00:02:37.367
Then a burst of 15 arrives.

41
00:02:37.367 --> 00:02:43.903
The time gone by is 5,000 milliseconds: 10 tokens earned, and the jar is full again.

42
00:02:43.903 --> 00:02:44.759
Ten get in.

43
00:02:46.800 --> 00:02:48.584
This is a token bucket.

44
00:02:48.584 --> 00:02:50.291
The jar is the bucket.

45
00:02:50.667 --> 00:02:51.552
Two details.

46
00:02:51.552 --> 00:02:59.000
The whole calculation runs as one script inside Redis, so two servers never both take the last token.

47
00:02:59.233 --> 00:03:03.920
And the key gets a TTL of 5 seconds, the time to fill an empty jar.

48
00:03:03.920 --> 00:03:09.167
After 5 quiet seconds the jar would be full anyway, so Redis may forget it.

49
00:03:09.367 --> 00:03:14.993
There's a version with only one number, called GCRA, the generic cell rate algorithm.

50
00:03:14.993 --> 00:03:23.332
It keeps a schedule instead of a jar: the time the client's next request would be due, if it sent at exactly the allowed rate.

51
00:03:23.500 --> 00:03:28.002
Each request that gets in pushes that time 500 milliseconds later.

52
00:03:28.002 --> 00:03:32.708
A request is refused if it would push it more than 10 steps past now.

53
00:03:33.067 --> 00:03:40.321
In the lesson's demo, the bucket and GCRA got the same traffic, and agreed on every single request.

54
00:03:40.321 --> 00:03:43.619
The bucket keeps two numbers, GCRA keeps one.

55
00:03:43.833 --> 00:03:45.299
When should you use one?

56
00:03:45.299 --> 00:03:51.347
When you want to cap how often a customer's program calls your service, and still allow its bursts.

57
00:03:51.347 --> 00:03:56.845
For a small exact limit, like attempts per card, the sliding log from earlier fits better.

58
00:03:57.033 --> 00:04:06.391
In Java and Kotlin, the call is the same: run the script on the client's key, with 10 tokens, 500 milliseconds, and the time now.

59
00:04:06.391 --> 00:04:07.841
A 1 means let it in.

60
00:04:08.367 --> 00:04:14.283
So, a burst, then a steady rate: the jar's size is the burst, and its refill is the rate.

61
00:04:14.467 --> 00:04:16.491
And the jar never needs a timer.

62
00:04:16.491 --> 00:04:20.731
It only needs to remember how full it was, and when it last looked.

63
00:04:21.100 --> 00:04:22.307
One quick check.

64
00:04:22.307 --> 00:04:29.018
A program empties its jar, then stays quiet for 2 seconds, and sends 10 requests at once.

65
00:04:29.018 --> 00:04:30.224
How many get in?

66
00:04:30.224 --> 00:04:31.431
Pause and think.

67
00:04:34.667 --> 00:04:35.070
Four.

68
00:04:35.070 --> 00:04:42.403
Two seconds is 2,000 milliseconds: four tokens earned, so four get in, and six are refused.

69
00:04:42.600 --> 00:04:45.190
Next, the customer who pressed Pay twice.

70
00:04:45.190 --> 00:04:49.739
A limit lets both presses through, so we'll learn to recognize a repeat.
